--- a/README.rst
+++ b/README.rst
@@ -45,6 +45,8 @@ installing Flaskr::
 Run
 ---
 
+For a new installation, initialize an empty database and start the app:
+
 .. code-block:: text
 
     $ flask --app flaskr init-db
@@ -53,13 +55,49 @@ Run
 Open http://127.0.0.1:5000 in a browser.
 
 
+Upgrade an existing database
+----------------------------
+
+Before starting this version against an existing Flaskr database, stop the app,
+back up the SQLite database, and run the migration using the same application
+configuration::
+
+    $ python -m flask --app flaskr upgrade-db
+
+The command uses the configured ``DATABASE`` path. By default this is
+``instance/flaskr.sqlite``; a custom path can be set in ``instance/config.py``.
+It adds ``post.status`` in place without dropping or recreating tables. Existing
+posts become ``published`` and retain their IDs, authors, creation times, titles,
+and bodies. Users, credentials, indexes, and other database data are preserved.
+The migration can be run repeatedly: existing statuses, including drafts, stay
+unchanged after the first upgrade. An uninitialized database reports an error;
+use ``init-db`` only for a new database. ``init-db`` clears existing data and must
+not be used to upgrade an installation.
+
+
+Drafts and publishing
+---------------------
+
+The New Post and Edit forms offer ``Published`` and ``Draft`` states. Only the
+author sees their drafts on the homepage, marked ``(Draft)``. Anonymous visitors
+and other users see only published posts. Posts keep their existing newest-first
+ordering, and only the author may edit or delete either kind of post.
+
+The ``status`` form field accepts exactly ``draft`` or ``published``. Creating a
+post without that field publishes it, preserving older form submissions. Editing
+without the field keeps the post's current status. Authors can publish a draft
+or return a published post to draft through the Edit form. Invalid values display
+a validation error and save no changes. The database also rejects invalid or
+null statuses, and inserts that omit the column default to ``published``.
+
+
 Test
 ----
 
 ::
 
     $ pip install '.[test]'
-    $ pytest
+    $ python -m pytest
 
 Run with coverage report::
 
